---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import GamePlayer from '../../components/GamePlayer.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import games from '../../data/games.json';
import skills from '../../data/skills.json';
import { url } from '../../config';

const game = games.find(g => g.id === 'pattern-repeat-k')!;
const skill = skills[game.skill as keyof typeof skills];
const page = new URL(url('logic-games/pattern-repeat-for-kindergarten/'), Astro.site).href;
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'LearningResource', name: `${game.keywordTitle}: ${game.title}`,
    url: page, description: game.summary, isAccessibleForFree: true, inLanguage: 'en',
    learningResourceType: 'Educational game', educationalLevel: 'Kindergarten', typicalAgeRange: '5-6',
    teaches: 'Seeing and continuing repeating patterns', timeRequired: 'PT3M', image: new URL(url(game.poster), Astro.site).href,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Memory games', item: new URL(url('logic-games/'), Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: game.keywordTitle, item: page },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Pattern Games for Kindergarten: Free Color and Shape Pattern Game for Ages 5 to 6 | EduLearnLabs"
  description="A free pattern game for Kindergarten. Kids finish bead bracelets for a friendly spider by filling the gaps so the pattern keeps going. Builds early algebra thinking. Every bead has its own shape. No login."
  path="logic-games/pattern-repeat-for-kindergarten/" nav="logic" image={game.poster} jsonLd={jsonLd}>

  <Breadcrumbs back={{ name: 'Memory games', path: 'logic-games/' }}
    items={[{ name: 'Home', path: '' }, { name: 'Memory games', path: 'logic-games/' }, { name: game.keywordTitle, path: 'logic-games/pattern-repeat-for-kindergarten/' }]} />

  <h1>Pattern Games for Kindergarten</h1>
  <p class="lead" style="font-size:20px;max-width:44em">Play <strong>{game.title}</strong>: help a friendly spider finish
    bead bracelets by filling the gaps so the pattern keeps going. Five small bracelets, about 3 minutes, free.</p>

  <GamePlayer game={game} topDifficulty={skill.topDifficulty}
    posterAlt={`${game.title} game screen: a cozy craft room with a spider, a string of colorful beads with a gap, and a tray of beads in different colors and shapes`} />

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>What is Bead Parlor?</h2>
    <p>Bead Parlor is a quiet craft room game. A friendly spider is stringing bracelets for the children who visit. Each
      string has a gap or two, and a tray of beads waits below. Your child puts the right bead in each gap so the pattern
      keeps going. When the string is full, the pattern plays as a little tune, one bead after another, the bracelet is
      tied and it hops onto your child's own shelf.</p>

    <h2>What kids learn</h2>
    <p>Patterns are early algebra thinking. To continue red, blue, red, blue, a child has to notice that something repeats,
      find the part that repeats and predict what comes next. That is the same habit of mind they will later use to
      count by twos, spot a rule in a number list and see how a sentence is built. The first bracelets copy a short
      pattern. Later ones need your child to continue it, then fill gaps in the middle, with longer repeats and more
      kinds of beads.</p>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>Kindergarten children, usually ages 5 to 6, who like beads, colors and shapes. There is nothing to read. A short
      voice asks "What comes next?" and never names the colors, so your child works it out by looking. It works on a
      tablet, a computer or a phone held sideways.</p>

    <h2>How to play</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Press Play, then look at the string of beads.</li>
      <li>Drag a bead from the tray into a gap. You can also tap a bead and then tap the gap, or use the keyboard.</li>
      <li>A right bead clicks into place and plays its note. A bead that does not fit wobbles and hops back, with no buzzer and no red mark.</li>
      <li>Stuck? Hazel the owl can help in small steps: first the start of the repeat pulses, then the next gap glows, then the beads you still need glow softly. She never places the bead for your child.</li>
      <li>Fill every gap and the bracelet is finished. Your child always finishes it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Every bead has a shape</h2>
    <p>About 1 in 12 boys is color blind, so color alone would leave some children out. In Bead Parlor every color is
      paired with its own shape: a circle, a square, a triangle, a star, a diamond or a heart. A child can read the
      pattern by color, by shape or by both, and the game stays fair for everyone.</p>

    <h2>Your bracelet shelf</h2>
    <p>Every finished bracelet joins your child's shelf, even when it needed a few hints. The shelf is kept on this
      device only. There is no score, no timer and no failure screen, and nothing is ever taken away.</p>

    <h2>Why it helps</h2>
    <p>Spotting a repeating unit is a skill children use long before formal math. The game keeps each bracelet within
      reach: after a few bracelets finished without help, the patterns get longer, and after a few that needed a lot
      of help, they step back. It is one small part of early math thinking, best next to patterns you find together
      at home.</p>

    <h2>Tips for parents</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Say the pattern out loud together: "red, blue, red, blue, what comes next?"</li>
      <li>Make patterns with real things: spoons and forks, socks, blocks, claps and stomps.</li>
      <li>Trying a bead that does not fit is how children test a guess. Point to the string and ask which beads you can see.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">More to play: see all <a href={url('logic-games/')}>free memory and logic games</a>, find matching pairs in <a href={url('logic-games/memory-match-for-preschool/')}>Memory
      Games for Preschool and Kindergarten</a>, or practice counting with <a href={url('math-games/counting-for-preschool/')}>Counting Games for Preschool</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <AdSlot id="game-bottom" />
</Base>
